**AI energy efficiency — where the gains are, and where total demand still rises**

1 November 2026 · KnowledgeXpress Singapore 🕯️

### The core tension
**Per-task efficiency is improving fast. Aggregate electricity use is still climbing.**  
Cheaper intelligence per watt invites more tokens, agents, and clusters — a classic rebound (Jevons-style) pattern.

---

### Scale of demand (order-of-magnitude)
| Metric | Figure (approx.) | Source flavor |
|--------|----------------|---------------|
| Global data-centre electricity ~2024–25 | ~415–485 TWh (~1.5% of world electricity) | IEA-linked reporting |
| Projection ~2030 | ~945–950 TWh (~3% of world electricity) | IEA Base Case |
| AI-focused facilities | Grew ~50% in 2025; path toward **tripling** by 2030 in some summaries | IEA / secondary |
| GPU share of facility power | ~40% of peak; rest = cooling, conversion, networking, overhead | Epoch AI |

So AI is a **fast-growing slice**, still a **minor share** of global electricity — but locally it already stresses grids, water, and siting.

---

### Hardware: more work per watt, higher watts per chip
- **GPU TDP rising:** data-centre GPUs moved from hundreds of watts toward **1.4–1.8 kW class** boards, with roadmaps pointing higher; racks for AI can sit near **~120 kW** (e.g. GB200 NVL72-class) vs ~8–11 kW typical enterprise racks.
- **Compute per watt** still improves generation-to-generation (vendor claims of large jumps in tokens or throughput per MW — treat marketing multiples carefully; direction is real).  
- **Facility stack matters:** GPUs ≠ total load. Cooling + power conversion + fabric can dominate the residual. Liquid cooling and higher-voltage DC (e.g. 800 VDC proposals) aim at better **PUE** and fewer conversion losses; end-to-end measured gains are still being nailed down.

---

### Software & model levers (often the biggest near-term knobs)
| Lever | Idea |
|-------|------|
| **Right-sized models** | Don’t default to the largest model; selection/distillation can cut energy sharply for mature tasks |
| **Quantization / lower precision** | FP8, FP4-class formats: more throughput per joule at acceptable quality |
| **Batching, caching, sparsity** | Higher utilization; less idle silicon |
| **Energy-aware training** | Critical-path scheduling, mixed precision, power capping |
| **Workload placement** | Run when/where grid carbon intensity is low |

Research estimates suggest **model selection alone** could cut a large fraction of inference energy if widely applied — while always using SOTA for every task would inflate demand.

---

### Efficiency paradox (honesty ledger)
- Google-type reports: **energy per median text prompt** down by large factors year-over-year, while **token volume** and **company power** still rise.  
- Inference often dominates lifetime energy for deployed models; training is spiky but not the whole story.  
- **Performance ≠ energy:** some LLM “optimize the code” loops produce faster but **more energy-heavy** programs than human baselines — another residual to measure, not assume.

---

### Link to the green multi-agent model
Efficiency \(e_i\) (useful work per joule) is the firm’s private lever; ecological load \(G_i \propto C_i / e_i \times \kappa\) still needs **price or cap** or rebound eats the gain.  
**ZEM line:** publish joules and carbon per useful unit (token, task, scientific result) on the honesty ledger — not only benchmark scores on the working ledger.

**Bottom line:** AI energy efficiency is a **full-stack race** (chips → cooling → models → ops → grids). Winning on per-watt metrics is necessary; **without demand discipline and clean supply**, total footprint keeps rising.


